<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Majapahit Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Majapahit</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Majapahit Empire was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> maritime state based in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, founded in 1293 after the repulse of a Mongol expedition. Under the minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gajah-mada">Gajah Mada</a> and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hayam-wuruk">Hayam Wuruk</a> in the fourteenth century, it claimed authority across much of the Indonesian archipelago. It succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> as the leading power of the region before declining in the fifteenth and early sixteenth centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire.jpg" alt="Majapahit Empire" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: source: BP47Dhorifah, svg: Puck04 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naval_flag_of_Majapahit_Kingdom.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A trading state on the northwest coast of Borneo appears in Chinese records from the tenth century under the name Po-ni, which sent missions to the Song court, and in 1365 the Javanese poem Nagarakretagama listed Barune among the dependencies of <b>Majapahit</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brunei-sultanate">Brunei Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Cham merchants settled in Sulu from the 10th to the 13th centuries, where they were called Orang Dampuan; Champa traded with Ma-i and Butuan in the Philippines and with Srivijaya and later <b>Majapahit</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/champa">Champa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Strait of Malacca had been dominated by Srivijaya, centred on Palembang, until the Cholas weakened it in the 11th century, and by the end of the 13th by the Javanese Singhasari and <b>Majapahit</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malacca-sultanate">Malacca Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The court chronicles known as the Babad Tanah Jawi, compiled in the Kartasura and Surakarta period, shaped how later Javanese understood their past, presenting Mataram as the heir of <b>Majapahit</b> and Demak.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mataram-sultanate">Mataram Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The rise of the Javanese kingdoms of Kediri, then Singhasari and <b>Majapahit</b>, created a powerful rival centre that contested Srivijayan cultural and commercial influence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/srivijaya-empire">Srivijaya Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1357, according to the Javanese Pararaton and the later poem Kidung Sunda, King Linggabuana travelled to <b>Majapahit</b> with his daughter Dyah Pitaloka, who was to marry King Hayam Wuruk.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sunda-kingdom">Sunda Kingdom · Military</a></li></ul></section>
